#{[17592186407849 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=\n$$"] [17592186413322 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{7}+3}{{x}^{4}+6x+2}=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186425676 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7afa339233c7e21eda233f5b67ccf8be9e05fb4ce9484c98eaf59eb790e651ab.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}x=-2\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}3=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6956ec22cfed2d304cc5c2352cf1fdf4c795ea423e7a19c8e3a3e2dc843ed.png)"] [17592186425677 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a29065f008fac7d3fd32d52b2ddc0b7d9360ffd75475f6c169eeb26582d22d2.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-4x=-4(-2)=8\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{x}^{3}=(2)^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d750ad2f9ea2a85446b94f7b19009ef74eeea25b4ccd1bf8ff4f408a5df381e2.png)"] [17592186425678 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d30d760181c95dd0764b9959ca91febb34e94618bb147d8022dae5745c38a1a.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}((x+2)^{3}+3)=(0)^{3}+3=3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(x+1)=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcdf5654322a6deff9d7598590b4e0e1ace6150020b4944ad844976fb0f70230.png)"] [17592186425680 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/11f9718c9384ad2fea0bf3f5cc7da720559c512a5b3cebbc11ca966d5ec0f28b.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}2x=2(-3)=-6\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0212ece76aba288e88b0c4d9f2e0f6163f4fd1f594cee30e9ea5f764ebc5f69.png)"] [17592186436123 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$"] [17592186436124 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\n$$"] [17592186436125 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$"] [17592186436126 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$"] [17592186436127 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186436128 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$"] [17592186436129 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}=\n$$"] [17592186436130 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\n$$"] [17592186436289 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+h-{y}^{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2})}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436290 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)(y+h))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436291 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xh-{y}^{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2})}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436292 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)^{y}+{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436294 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3(x+h)^{2}y-3{x}^{2}y}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436295 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)^{y}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436296 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436297 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+h-{y}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2})}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436302 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xh-{y}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2})}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436303 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436305 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436307 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{h\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2})}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436309 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}y-3{x}^{2}(y+h)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436314 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436315 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}(y+h)+3{x}^{2}y}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436316 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3(x+h)^{2}y+3{x}^{2}y}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436317 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{y+h}+{x}^{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436318 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436320 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x(y+h))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436321 :answer/value "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}(y+h)-3{x}^{2}y}{h}\\right)"] [17592186436332 :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={x}^{y},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=(x+h)^{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{(x+h)^{y}-{x}^{y}}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186436334 :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+h-{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+h-{y}^{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{y}^{2})}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186436335 :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)y).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186475943 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=2$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=x+1$\n\nIII. The limit does not exist"] [17592186480697 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}),\\,y(0)=200\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$"] [17592186486356 :question/problem "Given that $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-18{x}^{2}}{12p{x}^{2}-3x+9}=-2,$  what is the value of $p?$"] [17592186486357 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{7{n}^{4}+3}{4n-5{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186486359 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(4n-1)(4n+1)}{n(7-16{n}^{2})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186486372 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{27}{{x}^{3}-8}.$"] [17592186486402 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3{n}^{2}-1)(3{n}^{2}+1)}{{n}^{2}(4-9{n}^{3})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186486407 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+1}{3{x}^{4}-x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186486408 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+9}{8{x}^{5}+x}=$"] [17592186486420 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(5n-3)(5n+3)}{n(2-25{n}^{2})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186503687 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3}.$"] [17592186503750 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+5x+3}{{x}^{4}-2}=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186504031 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]"] [17592186504092 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186504128 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186504155 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]"] [17592186504179 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186504195 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186504295 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186504311 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186504322 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186504327 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{k}{\\pi n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\pi n}]"] [17592186504399 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186504422 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186504429 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186504437 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186504460 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186504609 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$"] [17592186504613 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ).$"] [17592186504615 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+1)^{3}?$"] [17592186504619 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186504619 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$"] [17592186504622 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes."] [17592186504631 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)"] [17592186504643 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186504648 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+5}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186504654 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186504654 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=7{x}_{k}^{-3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=3.\n$$"] [17592186504658 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186504658 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}+1}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [17592186504667 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)"] [17592186504671 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."] [17592186504672 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]$"] [17592186504674 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{5-x}\\right)=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=\\infty \\,,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)"] [17592186504678 :question/worked-solution "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."] [17592186504686 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186504686 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=3\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186504689 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/0f/0fa9338fbefb3682249a585b85d287a25868a8f137547830adf6085dae85139a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\pi )=1.\n$$"] [17592186504700 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$"] [17592186504705 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ \n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=1$ and $x=3,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes."] [17592186507431 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186507463 :question/problem "![](images/72/72e1d1f1ea1aa635441da5503a49ac4d33d5ab0df62d48aa699debc7816df1c2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above? \n\nI. $\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h}=1$\n\nII. $\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=-0.5$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h}=1$"] [17592186507480 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}=$"] [17592186507501 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e03f453156d57af94b13b76e22dc366644b18f56acc5f9a5f4a2c005e6bec3f7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\nI. $\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}=-1$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}=-1$"] [17592186508039 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $e$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x\\,f(x)}{\\,f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}\\,}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186508060 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=$"] [17592186508069 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}.$"] [17592186508078 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\,f(x)+{x}^{2}-3\\,}{\\,g(x)+{x}^{2}-4\\,}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186508115 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=2.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186508911 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\right)}^{2x+2}.$"] [17592186508953 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}.$"] [17592186508959 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3x+1}{3x}\\right)}^{6x}.$"] [17592186508976 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2}\\right)}^{x-1}.$"]}
